decisions:
  - id: d-emit-and-process
    decision: Replace bare emitEvent calls with emitAndProcess that bundles policy check + emission + nomination processing
    rationale: Single call site ensures every event goes through the full ambient pipeline. No way to accidentally emit without policy enforcement.
  - id: d-fire-and-forget-hooks
    decision: Hook event emission uses CLI paradigm event emit with & (background) instead of importing the MCP nomination engine
    rationale: Hooks must be fast (<100ms). CLI writes directly to stream.jsonl with no nomination processing. Nominations are deferred to the next MCP tool call.
  - id: d-surfacing-config
    decision: Surfacing rules live in .paradigm/surfacing.yaml, not in agent files
    rationale: Surfacing is a project-level preference (the human decides what noise level they want), not an agent-level behavior. Separating it lets you mute agents without editing their identity.
  - id: d-journal-promotion
    decision: Auto-promote journal entries with pattern_discovered trigger and confidence >= 0.8 to notebooks
    rationale: Closes the learning loop mechanically. Without this, journal entries accumulate but never influence future sessions. Notebooks are what buildProfileEnrichment actually reads.
  - id: d-sse-over-polling
    decision: Platform ambient section uses SSE EventSource for events, polls only for nominations
    rationale: Events are high-frequency (every file edit). SSE gives real-time updates without 10-second latency. Nominations are low-frequency and don't need sub-second delivery.
learnings:
  - The nomination brief quality matters more than the scoring algorithm — a mechanical 'symbol match on X' brief is useless even if the score is perfect. Role-aware contextual briefs make nominations actionable.
  - Surfacing config is essential for adoption — without it, a noisy agent with a low threshold floods the human with irrelevant nominations and they disable the whole system.
  - Symphony relay integration was simpler than expected — writing to the outbox directory is enough, the relay's file watcher picks it up. No direct WebSocket API needed from the nomination engine.
  - The graduation pipeline connection (ambient → hooks) is the most architecturally significant piece — it means agent behaviors that prove reliable can eventually become automated enforcement, reducing the human-in-the-loop cost over time.
  - SSE with incremental byte reads (tracking file offset) is more efficient than re-parsing the entire JSONL on each poll cycle, but requires handling file truncation edge cases.
